  "query_preview": "..\nToday: Another round of breezy westerly winds look to develop \nthis afternoon and evening as another shortwave traverses the \nregion. This will again lead to elevated to locally critical \nfire weather conditions in the Wenatchee area and the Waterville\nPlateau with gusts around 20-35 mph and relative humidities \ngenerally between 15-25 percent. The wildcard remains smoke \nreducing temperatures in those areas, which would help reduce \nfire risk. Elsewhere, expect temperatures to cool a few degrees\ntoday under the influence of zonal flow aloft and another \npassing shortwave. Wildfire smoke and haze will continue to \nimpact the region, especially in the vicinity of or downwind of \nongoing wildfires. \n\nMonday and Tuesday: Zonal flow will continue through at least \nTuesday, bringing near normal temperatures to the region and \nbreezy winds to the Cascade gaps in the late afternoons and \nevenings. Localized smoke/haze may bring temperatures down a \nfew degrees each day Fire conditions will remain elevated due to\nlow RH and breezy winds, with winds increasing each afternoon \nand evening.\n\nWednesday through Sunday: Confidence in the forecast decreases \nheading into the second half of the week as a broad shortwave \ntrough sinks down from British Columbia. This system may bring \nthe first chance of precipitation in a few weeks to northern \nareas near the Canadian border by Wednesday. While there is a \n15-25% chance of showers, preciptiation continues to looks to \nremain rather weak with this system and may not provide any real\nwetting rains at this time. Additionally, there is a slight \nchance of (10-20%) of thunderstorms in northeast WA and northern\nID both Wednesday and Thursday afternoon and evenings. Quasi- \nzonal flow will then briefly return on Friday as the trough to \nthe north exits the area. Ensemble guidance remains indicative \nof an upper level trough developing along the coast over the \nweekend and bringing southwest flow aloft to the area. This \ncould continue to raise further concerns for thunderstorms \nacross the region by the weekend.",
